---
name: pdf-reader
description: Read and comprehend PDF files (and DOCX/PPTX/XLSX), especially math lecture notes and academic papers. Use when the user asks to read, parse, analyze, or extract content from a PDF or document file.
---

# PDF Reader

Use the `liteparse` CLI. It parses the text layer to markdown and has built-in OCR (Indra) for scanned, rotated or image-only pages, in most scripts. DOCX/PPTX/XLSX need LibreOffice.

Work in `/tmp/pi-pdf/<name>/` (`mkdir -p` it). Parse once, then grep the file; don't re-parse to look things up.

## 1. Triage: which pages need OCR

```bash
liteparse is-complex FILE -q | jq -r '.[] | "\(.page_number)\t\(.needs_ocr)\t\(.reasons|join(","))"' > pages.tsv
```

Columns: page, needs OCR, reasons. A non-zero exit status only means some page was flagged.

- Every page `false`, or only `sparse-text` → no OCR needed.
- Any `scanned`, `no-text`, `vector-text`, `embedded-images` or `garbled` → use OCR.

## 2. Parse once

```bash
liteparse parse FILE --format markdown -q --no-ocr -o doc.md   # no OCR needed
liteparse parse FILE --format markdown -q -o doc.md            # OCR (runs only on pages that lack text)
```

Pages are separated by lines of `-----`. Parse the whole file so page numbers line up (or add `--target-pages "1-5,9"` for a part).

## 3. Find and read

```bash
grep -n '^#' doc.md                                          # outline
awk '/^-----$/{p++} /REGEX/{print p+1": "$0}' doc.md         # search, with page numbers
awk -v n=7 '/^-----$/{p++; next} p+1==n' doc.md              # read page 7
```

Short documents (≤15 pages): read all of `doc.md`. Longer: outline and search, then read the relevant pages (and the next one when a proof or table continues).

## Caveats

- A page whose text is all rotated (sideways scan, landscape page) is empty in markdown: `liteparse parse FILE --format text -q --target-pages N`.
- OCR'd pages can have misread characters and no heading/table structure; say so when quoting them.
- Math is often garbled: reconstruct it in LaTeX and flag what was unreadable. Figures: only their text and captions come through.
- Always cite page numbers.
